  "body": "Asked for by the maintainer in chat, 2026-11-16:\n\n> \"caching to not have to redraw things should prob. be a strategy thru out the application to keep framerates stable and enough compute lefter over for things that matter.. Precalculation and cache is not something that will give us a penalty.. this is not game engine\"\n\nand, for the frame time: \"the performance needs to gget down under 10ms to be useable\".\n\nWhat is wanted: one strategy for the full application. A frame does not calculate again what did not change. Derived data (chains, stitched rings, overlay geometry, panel rows, meshes, simulation views) is calculated one time, stored, and made invalid by a change of its inputs. A wait for a calculation before the display is acceptable.\n\nToday: #2201, #2209 and #2235 each repair one place where a frame does work in proportion to the model or the selection. Each repair has its own cache and its own invalidation rule.\n\nWork, in this order:\n\n1. Measure. List the work that each frame does in `commands`, `scene` and `ui` on a large model (`fig8.step` and one larger), with a probe that stays in the test suite.\n2. Design one cache rule: the key (a model revision, a selection revision, a document revision), who makes an entry invalid, and where the entries are stored (app layer, not UI widgets). Record it in `docs/decisions/` and in `crates/ui/AGENTS.md`.\n3. Idle frames: when no input and no change exists, the app does not repaint, or it repaints from stored buffers.\n4. Move each measured hot path to the rule, one sub-issue for each.\n5. A budget test: a frame without a change is below 10 ms on the reference models. The gate runs it.\n\nSafety: a cache must never show or cut stale geometry. Each cache has a test that a change of its inputs makes it invalid. The verify check and the post gate read the document, not a display cache.\n\nDone means: the decision is recorded, the budget test is in the gate, and the sub-issues for the measured hot paths are filed. #2235 gives the first cache and the first probe.\n",
